userc     = {https://ctan.org/tex-archive/obsolete/macros/latex/contrib/oops},
abstract  = {(no language specified) oops is a package for LaTeX (hence ''scribe'') that organizes (typically mathematical) definitions along 
            two dimensions: functions and objects, hence ''OO''.

            Such definitions are made inline thanks to a minimalist interface built upon xparse. To make a definition, 
            use  \texttt{{\textbackslash}OopsNew{\textbraceleft}\&lt;tl1\&gt;{\textbraceright}} , 
            where \texttt{\&lt;tl1\&gt;} identifies an object, followed by input that alternates 
            between ''text'' and instructions. The latter create and expand definitions using rules that can be modified 
            at the package level or themselves inline. This framework is suitable for instance where \texttt{\&lt;tl1\&gt;} is 
            either of ModelA and ModelB, and each requires its own definition of, say, a space. In this case, they 
            would be encoded respectively as \texttt{{\textbackslash}Space{\textbraceleft}ModelA{\textbraceright}} and \texttt{{\textbackslash}Space{\textbraceleft}ModelB{\textbraceright}} . 
            However, this would be verbose if most functions applied to just one object, so the package provides 
            a generic one that is set by default to Math. For example, \texttt{{\textbackslash}OopsNew{\textbraceleft}Math{\textbraceright}[Let{\textasciitilde}]{\textbraceleft}Space={\textbackslash}Omega{\textbraceright}[{\textasciitilde}denote 
            the sample space]{\textbraceleft}{\textbraceright}} , followed by \texttt{\${\textbackslash}Space\$} , 
            expand to: ''Let Omega denote the sample space'' and ''Omega''.

            Other features automate repetitive formatting tasks. Altogether, ''practical''.

            As of April 2020, this package has been superseded by the  \href{https://ctan.org/pkg/ccool}{ccool} package.},
year      = {2020},
language  = {no language specified},
keywords  = {maths},
usera     = {Erwann Rogard (2020)},
userb     = {lppl1.3c (free)},
userd     = {repository: https://github.com/rogard/oops},
related   = {Rogard2020.a},
note      = {Documentation (no language specified): Readme: pkg:/README.md;
