            and \texttt{xstring} packages.

            Commands that have different definitions come with PT in the beginning of their name (e.g.  \texttt{{\textbackslash}PTmqty} ).

            \texttt{physics-patch} has covered all commands in \href{https://ctan.org/pkg/physics}{physics} since 
            version 2.0, so there’s no need to load \texttt{physics} .

            It is ok to load  \texttt{physics} before this package. This package will silently override 
            macros in \texttt{physics} with an improved version. To use the original version provided 
            by \texttt{physics} , load \texttt{physics} before this package and use the \texttt{nooverride} option 
            (not recommended).

            This package pretends that the  \texttt{physics} package is loaded so that this package won’t 
            be overridden if loading \texttt{physics} is called afterwards and packages loaded afterwards 
            that check whether \texttt{physics} is loaded to determine their behavior (e.g. \texttt{siunitx} ) 
            work correctly. To disable this, use the \texttt{nopretend} option (not recommended).

            If  \href{https://ctan.org/pkg/siunitx}{siunitx} is loaded before this package, this 
            package will define \texttt{{\textbackslash}ITquantity} and \texttt{{\textbackslash}ITqty} as 
            the integration of the improved definition of \texttt{physics} ’s \texttt{{\textbackslash}qty} (in \texttt{{\textbackslash}PHquantity} and \texttt{{\textbackslash}PHqty} ) 
